FUNCTION HTTPGET(URL)
* mvx-lang/curl-cmd — HTTP GET returning the response body, via the OS `curl`
* command (the "command" HTTP transport; the libcurl in-process version lives in
* mvx-lang/curl).  curl-cmd provides the virtual name "curl", so it satisfies a
* dependency on "curl" with no compiler or libcurl needed — only the curl
* binary, which mvpkg already requires.  GPL-2.0-only.
$INCLUDE MVPKG.INC PLATFORM.H
*
* NO MVX ARM, DELIBERATELY (#20).  mvpkg.json declares udt, uv and jbase, and
* mvx is served by the runtime's own http extension (HTTPGET / HTTPGETFILE /
* HTTPPOST, built in since mvx#286) with mvx-lang/curl for TLS.  There used to
* be $IFDEF MVX arms here; nothing ever compiled them, and every one had rotted:
* they called OSEXEC with a shell command line where mvx takes an FM-delimited
* argv and runs no shell, read the captured output from the RETURN value where
* mvx returns the exit status and captures into a second argument, and wrote
* OSWRITE(path, content) where mvx takes (content, path) and answers in its
* return rather than STATUS().  HTTPGET's arm read a temp file no MVX branch
* ever wrote.  A port that is never compiled is not support; it is debris that
* reads like support.
*
* -o RATHER THAN `>`.  Redirection is a SHELL feature, and jBASE's EXECUTE runs
* an OS command directly with no shell to interpret it -- `> file` would arrive
* as two more arguments to curl.  curl writes the file itself with -o, which
* needs no shell anywhere and is one less thing to quote.
   TMP = "/tmp/curlcmd_get_" : @USERNO
$IFDEF JBASE
* NO SHELL MEANS NO QUOTING.  jBASE splits the command itself and passes the
* pieces through, quotes included -- so a quoted URL reaches curl WITH its
* quotes and fails.  Unquoted is correct here for the same reason it would be
* wrong anywhere else.
   EXECUTE 'curl -sL -o ' : TMP : ' ' : URL CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
   EXECUTE '!curl -sL -o "' : TMP : '" "' : URL : '"' CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
* THE QUOTES ARE BUILT, NOT WRITTEN.  UniVerse needs SH -c, so the whole curl
* command is one argument to sh and has to survive TWO parsers.  Spelling that
* inline mixes ' and " until neither balances -- the previous version emitted
*     SH -c 'curl -sL -o /tmp/f "https://...'
* with the double quote never closed, so curl saw no URL, wrote nothing, and
* HTTPGET returned "" with no error anywhere.  CHAR(34)/CHAR(39) make the
* nesting explicit: double quotes around the sh command, single around the URL.
   UDQ = CHAR(34) ; USQ = CHAR(39)
   EXECUTE "SH -c " : UDQ : "curl -sL -o " : TMP : " " : USQ : URL : USQ : UDQ CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
   BODY = ""
* UniVerse has no OSREAD -- it parses `OSREAD X FROM p` as a variable being
* assigned, and says so as "Variable Name (UNDEFINED) unexpected".  OPENSEQ /
* READSEQ is the equivalent there.  Flat guards, because an $IFDEF nested in an
* $ELSE compiles on UniData and fails on UniVerse.
$IFDEF UV
   OPENSEQ TMP TO CFV THEN
      CDONE = 0
      LOOP UNTIL CDONE DO
         READSEQ CLINE FROM CFV THEN
            IF BODY = "" THEN BODY = CLINE ELSE BODY := CHAR(10) : CLINE
         END ELSE CDONE = 1
      REPEAT
      CLOSESEQ CFV
   END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF JBASE
   OSREAD BODY FROM TMP ELSE BODY = ""
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
   OSREAD BODY FROM TMP ELSE BODY = ""
$ENDIF
$IFDEF JBASE
   EXECUTE 'rm -f ' : TMP CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
   EXECUTE '!rm -f "' : TMP : '"' CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
   EXECUTE "SH -c " : CHAR(34) : "rm -f " : TMP : CHAR(34) CAPTURING COUT
$ENDIF
   RETURN(BODY)
